     "text": "```python\nfrom pedantic import timer_class, retry, require_kwargs, count_calls\nfrom typing import List, Any, Optional\n\n@timer_class\n@retry(max_retries=3, delay=1)\n@require_kwargs\n@count_calls\ndef find_kth_largest_element(arr: List[Any], k: int) -> Optional[Any]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the kth largest element in the given array.\n\n    Args:\n        arr (List[Any]): The input array.\n        k (int): The position of the element to find (1-based index).\n\n    Returns:\n        Optional[Any]: The kth largest element, or None if k is out of bounds.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not arr or k <= 0 or k > len(arr):\n        return None\n\n    # Sort the array in ascending order\n    sorted_arr = sorted(arr)\n\n    # Return the kth largest element\n    return sorted_arr[-k]\n```\n\n### Explanation of Coding Practices Applied:\n\n- **`@timer_class`**: Ensures that the execution time of the class is tracked, aiding in performance analysis.\n- **`@retry`**: Adds robustness by retrying the method up to 3 times if an exception occurs, improving fault tolerance.\n- **`@require_kwargs`**: Enforces that all required keyword arguments are provided when the method is called, enhancing code reliability.\n- **`@count_calls`**: Tracks the number of times the method is invoked, which is useful for debugging and monitoring usage patterns.\n- **Type Hints**: The function uses type annotations for `List[Any]` and `Optional[Any]` to improve code clarity and maintainability.\n- **Docstring**: A detailed docstring is included to document the function's purpose, arguments, return value, and behavior.\n- **Edge Case Handling**: The function checks for invalid input (empty array, k out of bounds) and returns `None` in such cases, ensuring robustness and preventing unexpected errors.\n- **Sorting Logic**: The function sorts the array in ascending order and returns the kth largest element using negative indexing, which is a standard and efficient approach.",
